<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <WarningLevel>3</WarningLevel>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <DefineConstants>FASTSERIALIZATION_PUBLIC;STREAMREADER_PUBLIC</DefineConstants>
    <!--
      Workstation GC's gen0 budget is sized adaptively from recent allocation
      rate. This tool reads a whole capture into one big byte[] up front then
      holds a single List<EventRecord> (a struct, ~70 bytes/element - one
      ~1GB contiguous array for a real 5-minute/14.8M-event capture) alive
      across every projection pass. A full/gen2 GC landing while that array
      is live has to scan (and potentially compact) the whole thing - under
      Workstation GC this was measured adding 1-2+ SECONDS to
      GcEventProjector.Project alone (a loop that otherwise runs in ~25ms),
      because the array is large enough relative to Workstation GC's
      allocation-rate-adaptive gen0 budget to provoke a full collection mid-run.
      Server GC's much larger, throughput-oriented segment sizing avoided
      triggering any gen0/1/2 collection at all during the same run -
      verified via GC.CollectionCount before/after. Appropriate here since
      this is a short-lived batch CLI process, not a latency-sensitive
      service where Server GC's larger memory footprint would be a concern.
    -->
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <!--
      Was false (a deliberate earlier choice, per this section's own
      comment above about why Server GC alone was already worth turning
      on). Re-measured via dotnet-trace's gc-verbose profile on a real
      capture with today's caller-tree drill-down work in place (which
      allocates far more total garbage than the code this comment
      originally described): 5 GCs, up to 580ms each, 1.26 SECONDS of
      total pause time out of a ~3.3-3.7s run - roughly a third of the
      whole process blocked in stop-the-world collection, since
      Concurrent=false means every gen2 GC is fully blocking with no
      background overlap. Server GC's own concurrent (background) mode
      lets a gen2 collection's mark phase run mostly alongside the
      mutator instead - worth the small per-allocation write-barrier
      overhead given how much blocking pause time is on the table here.
    -->
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      ClrMD is the supported API over the DAC - the same component
      `dotnet-dump analyze` and SOS read a dump through. This is deliberately
      NOT the same situation as the .nettrace reader's refusal to depend on
      TraceEvent: that one decodes a documented file format, which is a
      reasonable thing to hand-roll and has paid for itself in bugs found.
      A core dump is read by asking a versioned, private runtime contract
      questions, and there is no hand-rolled version of that worth writing.
      Only CoreDump/ touches this package; every other path in this tool still
      parses its input itself.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Diagnostics.Runtime" Version="3.1.512801" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
